"""Employing Numbers — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter Five — Employing Numbers'
HEAD = '''Seven passages, 265 characters: the shortest chapter, and the one most like the <i>Art of War</i> — from beginning to end it is about numbers, ground, reading the enemy and pursuit, and it does not once mention benevolence. “When you bring many against few, encircle them at a distance and leave a gap” is Sunzi’s “leave a way out to a surrounded army.” “Use many and few to see how he shifts; advance and withdraw to see how firm he is,” and six such tests running, followed by eight things to do about what you have seen — the only stretch of pure tactics in the book. The last passage is the hardest in it: cut off letters from home, pick out the best men and re-sort the weapons, throw away the baggage and cut the rations. “Letters from home are cut off: this is called cutting off the thought of looking back.” And there the book ends, on the same four words that close three other chapters — <i>such was the government of antiquity</i> — the first sentence of the first chapter having been “in antiquity benevolence was taken as the root.”'''

PARA = {
 'p5-1': 'The way of war: with few, firmness; with many, order. Few profit by intricacy, many by regularity. With many, advance and halt; with few, advance and withdraw. When you bring many against few, encircle them at a distance and leave a gap. If the enemy divides and strikes by turns, then meet the many with few. If the multitude are uncertain, act on your own judgment. If you have the advantage, lower the flags, go to meet him and drive him back. If the enemy is many, hold together and accept the encirclement; if he is few, or if he is afraid, avoid him and open a way for him. (“Leave a gap” is to surround on three sides and leave one open, as in Sunzi. “Lower the flags” is to take down the colours so that the enemy cannot read your strength. For “hold together” both witnesses have a graph meaning “take the measure of his numbers”; the base text makes sense and is kept.)',
 'p5-2': 'In war: keep the wind behind you and the high ground behind you; the heights on your right and the difficult ground on your left; pass quickly over the marsh and quickly over the broken ground; when you halt twice in the same place, choose ground that falls away on all sides like the back of a turtle. (The marsh is the water-and-reed country; the “broken ground” is ground that has given way (one edition writes the graph differently). The turtle’s back is ground raised in the middle so that water runs off it. The whole passage is terrain, and may be read beside the “Marches” chapter of the <i>Art of War</i>.)',
 'p5-3': 'In war: deploy, and watch what he does; watch the enemy, and then act. If you are to wait, go along with him and do not beat the drum; wait for his men to move. If you are to attack, encamp and watch for him. (“Go along with him” is not to provoke him. Whether you wait or attack, you look first.)',
 'p5-4': 'In war: use many and few to see how he shifts; advance and withdraw to see how firm he is; endanger him and see whether he is afraid; be still and see whether he grows slack; move and see whether he is uncertain; raid him and see whether he is in order. Then: strike where he is uncertain; press him where he is caught short; drive him to where he has no case; raid him where he has laid his plans; take him where he cannot get out of the way; block his design; take away his calculation; and come down on him while he is afraid. (Six acts of looking, then eight of striking. The Siku text has a different graph in “while he is afraid.” This is the passage in the book that comes nearest to the <i>Art of War</i>.)',
 'p5-5': 'In pursuit of a routed enemy, do not stop. If the enemy halts somewhere on the road, think again. (The base text has “halts, not the road,” which is not Chinese; both witnesses read “halts on the road,” and it is emended here. Press on without pause, and be suspicious of a halt: fourteen characters, one loosening and one tightening.)',
 'p5-6': 'When you are close to an enemy’s capital there must be a road in; and if you withdraw, you must have thought out the way back. (The Siku text writes the word for “back” differently. Thirteen characters that say everything about going deep alone.)',
 'p5-7': 'In war: to be first is to wear yourself out, to be second is to be cowed; to rest is to grow slack, not to rest is also to wear yourself out, and to rest too long is to come back to being cowed. Letters from home are cut off: this is called cutting off the thought of looking back. The best men are picked out and the weapons re-sorted: this is called adding to men’s strength. The baggage is thrown away and the rations cut: this is called opening men’s minds. Such was the government of antiquity. (“Letters from home” is correspondence with one’s family; the baggage is what the men must carry. The three measures grow harder in turn — no letters, a thinning of men and gear, no baggage and short rations — until there is only one way left to go. The book closes on “such was the government of antiquity,” and its first sentence was “in antiquity benevolence was taken as the root.”)',
}
